Below is a **self‑contained C# console program** that demonstrates

* how to declare a custom attribute,
* how to apply that attribute to classes, methods, properties and even the assembly,
* and how to read those attributes at runtime using **reflection**.

```csharp
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//  AttributesAndReflectionDemo.cs
// ------------------------------------------------------------

using System;
using System.Reflection;

// 1. Declare a custom attribute.
//    - AttributeTargets specifies where it can be applied.
//    - Inherited = false  → the attribute is not inherited by derived types.
//    - AllowMultiple = false → only one instance per target.
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class |
                AttributeTargets.Method |
                AttributeTargets.Property |
                AttributeTargets.Assembly,
                Inherited = false,
                AllowMultiple = false)]
public sealed class MyInfoAttribute : Attribute
{
    // Positional parameters (must be supplied in the constructor).
    public string Author { get; }
    public string Version { get; }

    // Named parameters (settable properties) – optional.
    public string Description { get; set; }

    public MyInfoAttribute(string author, string version)
    {
        Author = author;
        Version = version;
    }
}

// 2. Apply the attribute to the *assembly*.
//    This must appear before any namespace declaration or class definition.
[assembly: MyInfo("Charlie", "2.0", Description = "Demo assembly")]

namespace Demo
{
    // 3. Apply to a class.
    [MyInfo("Alice", "1.0", Description = "Sample class")]
    public class SampleClass
    {
        // 4. Apply to a property.
        [MyInfo("Alice", "1.0")]
        public string SampleProperty { get; set; } = "Hello";

        // 5. Apply to a method.
        [MyInfo("Bob", "1.1", Description = "Prints a message")]
        public void SampleMethod()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Inside SampleMethod");
        }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // ---------------------------------
            // 6.  Read assembly attribute.
            // ---------------------------------
            var asm = Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly();
            var asmAttr = asm.GetCustomAttribute<MyInfoAttribute>();
            Console.WriteLine($"Assembly: Author={asmAttr.Author}, Version={asmAttr.Version}, Desc={asmAttr.Description}");

            // ---------------------------------
            // 7.  Read class attribute.
            // ---------------------------------
            Type type = typeof(SampleClass);
            var classAttr = type.GetCustomAttribute<MyInfoAttribute>();
            Console.WriteLine($"Class {type.Name}: Author={classAttr.Author}, Version={classAttr.Version}, Desc={classAttr.Description}");

            // ---------------------------------
            // 8.  Read property attribute.
            // ---------------------------------
            PropertyInfo prop = type.GetProperty(nameof(SampleClass.SampleProperty));
            var propAttr = prop.GetCustomAttribute<MyInfoAttribute>();
            Console.WriteLine($"Property {prop.Name}: Author={propAttr.Author}, Version={propAttr.Version}");

            // ---------------------------------
            // 9.  Read method attribute.
            // ---------------------------------
            MethodInfo meth = type.GetMethod(nameof(SampleClass.SampleMethod));
            var methAttr = meth.GetCustomAttribute<MyInfoAttribute>();
            Console.WriteLine($"Method {meth.Name}: Author={methAttr.Author}, Version={methAttr.Version}, Desc={methAttr.Description}");

            // ---------------------------------
            // 10.  Enumerate *all* members that have MyInfoAttribute
            // ---------------------------------
            Console.WriteLine("\nAll members with MyInfoAttribute:");
            foreach (var member in type.GetMembers())
            {
                var attr = member.GetCustomAttribute<MyInfoAttribute>();
                if (attr != null)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"  {member.MemberType,-10} {member.Name,-20} Author={attr.Author} Version={attr.Version}");
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What the program does

1. **Custom Attribute**  
   `MyInfoAttribute` contains two *positional* parameters (`Author`, `Version`) and one *named* property (`Description`).

2. **Applying the Attribute**  
   The attribute is applied at four levels:
   * **Assembly** – `[assembly: MyInfo(...)]`
   * **Class** – `[MyInfo(...)]`
   * **Property** – `[MyInfo(...)]`
   * **Method** – `[MyInfo(...)]`

3. **Reflection**  
   * `Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly()` → get the assembly and read its attribute.
   * `Type.GetCustomAttribute<T>()` → read the attribute applied to a class.
   * `PropertyInfo.GetCustomAttribute<T>()` → read attribute on a property.
   * `MethodInfo.GetCustomAttribute<T>()` → read attribute on a method.
   * Finally, `Type.GetMembers()` is used to loop through *all* members of a type and print those that have the attribute.

Running this program will print something like:

```
Assembly: Author=Charlie, Version=2.0, Desc=Demo assembly
Class SampleClass: Author=Alice, Version=1.0, Desc=Sample class
Property SampleProperty: Author=Alice, Version=1.0
Method SampleMethod: Author=Bob, Version=1.1, Desc=Prints a message

All members with MyInfoAttribute:
  Class      SampleClass         Author=Alice Version=1.0
  Property   SampleProperty       Author=Alice Version=1.0
  Method     SampleMethod         Author=Bob  Version=1.1
```

Feel free to modify the attribute definition, add more members, or use the attributes in other ways (e.g., to change program behavior at runtime). This example shows the core pattern: **define → apply → read via reflection**.
